# Crew rosters - who is at a console when nobody typed a name.
#
# The library owns the MECHANISM (sbs_utils.procedural.crew); this addon owns the CONTENT.
# That split is why a mod can supply its own crew for its own hulls without going near
# LegendaryMissions, and why a mission can pick one of these from the server console's Crew
# dropdown or pin one with `Defaults: CREW_SELECT` on its @map.
#
# Loading this addon changes nothing on its own. The rosters are OFFERED - not one of them
# declares a `Hull:` or a `Ship:` - so with CREW_SELECT unset (the library default) every
# console still reads exactly as it did before: whatever the player typed, or nothing.
provides crew.rosters

# ONE top-level `shared` line, the shape `comms/enemy_taunt.mast` already uses for its hails.
# Top-level `shared` runs once for the whole story rather than once per connecting client,
# which is what a registry write wants - three rosters registered five times is five times
# the work and the same answer.
#
# The file text is passed STRAIGHT THROUGH rather than parked in a variable first: a MAST
# assignment re-runs a string through f-string formatting, so a roster that ever contained a
# brace would become a syntax error reported against this line.
#
# `crew_load_amd` is the mission-relative form and cannot see inside a mastlib, so an addon
# reads its own file out of its own zip - the same shape sides_declare_amd needs, and for the
# same reason.
shared CREW_ROSTERS = crew_declare_amd(amd_document(media_read_relative_file("crew_rosters.amd"), data_parser=amd_crew_data))
